dsh: reasoning:
I see that the supervisor launches a task in a tmux session and waits 20 seconds before reporting counts, but it doesn't immediately relaunch completed tasks—instead it loops every 20 seconds and attempts to launch each role, checking if a tmux session already exists to skip duplicates. Tracing back to the tmux session name for worker-041, I can see from the process list that the DSH process with PID 2101801 is running in that session, which was started at 01:18:30. So when I check the tmux session status, it exists and the launch function returns early without creating a new one — but then how did I end up with multiple instances at 01:18:30, 01:11, and earlier? It seems `has-session` will fail if the dsh exits but leaves the tmux session behind, which would cause relaunches on each loop iteration.

Actually, more likely: if the tmux session persists as "dead" after the dsh exits, then subsequent `has-session` calls would succeed and no new launch would occur. But if the tmux server itself dies or the session closes, then a relaunch happens. The real issue is that the supervisor has no completion detection — it just keeps looping and relaunching whenever the tmux session disappears. It can't distinguish between a task that finished successfully and one that crashed. The proper fix would be to have the supervisor track completion markers or exit codes so it knows not to relaunch completed work.

